.TH EUCLID-CLI-EKM-GET-CERTIFICATE 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-ekm-get-certificate \- show one certificate
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli ekm get-certificate
.BR \-\-name | \-n
.I NAME
.SH DESCRIPTION
Shows one certificate: its subject, issuer, serial number, fingerprint,
subject alternative names, validity period, and the PEM itself.
.PP
The PEM is included because it is public by construction \- every
client that opens a connection is handed a copy \- and because it is
what somebody needs in order to add a self-signed certificate to a
trust store, or to pass to
.B euclid-cli \-\-ca\-cert
when talking to a euclid that serves one.
.PP
The private key is not reported. It never leaves the server.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-name " " \fINAME\fR
Certificate name, within the caller's own account and namespace.
Required.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The certificate is printed as JSON.
.TP
.B 1
No certificate of that name exists, the server could not be reached, or
it rejected the request.
.SH EXAMPLES
Write a listener's certificate out for a client that has to trust it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli --endpoint https://localhost:5566 --pretty false \e
    ekm get-certificate --name eag-development \e
    | jq -r .certificate.certificate > eag-development.pem
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-ekm (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ekm-create-certificate (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ekm-import-certificate (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ekm-list-certificates (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ekm-delete-certificate (1)
